WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.040
Film Courage: This question is from viewer
Nolan Putnam “What is the most helpful habit

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.550
you’ve created (in regards to screenwriting)
that’s allowed you to become a professional?

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.589
Erik Bork, Screenwriter/Author: That’s a
great question, is there one habit that has

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:15.280
been the most helpful?

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:25.800
Probably we’ve talked about most of them,
probably the most important perspective has

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.699
been to see it as something I am always learning,
I’m always growing and that that’s okay.

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:34.410
It’s not like I’m supposed to know it
all and be perfect at it and the things that

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.250
I write are going to be great automatically
and others are going to love them.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:43.489
To see it more as it’s an ongoing quest
of kind of a self-development, self-education,

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:50.910
I’m learning with everything that I write
what works better and not as good so it’s

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:57.390
like this journey of development that you
sort of like are going to enjoy the journey

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.160
as opposed to I have to get to a certain place
which it’s easy to feel that way because

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.931
the world works that way where I have to get
the agent, I have to get the sale, I have

00:01:06.931 --> 00:01:10.210
to get something produced, I have to raise
my money and make my movie.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.720
Like there are all these goals that we might
have in the world and a lot of times they

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.780
are out of our control and if you focus only
on those goals like what I can get, what I

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.000
can achieve instead of what can I express?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.310
How can I just do what I decide I want to
do which is be a writer and keep learning

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.970
and growing and find some satisfaction in
that journey.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.040
Because when you turn it into there have to
be these quantifiable results in terms of

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.340
money or others reactions, whatever.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.340
Then you’re putting your power in this thing
that may or may not ever happen, you can’t

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.399
for sure make happen no matter what you do.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:51.549
So I think that attitude might seem counterproductive
because Well to be successful (professional)

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:55.200
you want to focus on the professional goals,
right?

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.590
Well you do to some extent you are open to
feedback and you are educating yourself.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:06.420
I didn’t say just go in a hole and do what
you want to do and never…and shut the outside

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:07.469
world out.

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:12.000
I’m saying engage with the world with feedback,
with education, with trying to understand

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.250
and get better so it’s growth but making
it a positive growth for yourself is about

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:22.610
making me better and making my writing better,
not making my results better.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.010
Because when you focus on how do I give more,
how do I write something that is really going

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.250
to impact people more, how do I that?

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.750
How do I figure out how to do it?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:35.950
How do I get better at it, you are then about
giving to other people and you will achieve

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:42.570
success I believe more when you succeed at
giving more.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:47.650
You get more automatically as opposed to how
do I get the success, how do I get the breaks,

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.840
how do I get the right person to read it,
how do I get people to like it?

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:56.960
It’s more how do I create something so wonderful
that people will just automatically like it,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.960
I’m not even concerning myself with that.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.030
So it’s like taking the power into your
own hands and being about how you can improve

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:11.400
your stuff, your self as a writer, having
that approach I think is a stronger healthier

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.070
approach that is going to lead to success
more then you do it the other way.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:20.220
Film Courage: That’s amazing because especially
to if you do live here in Los Angeles (I know

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:26.120
you said you don’t necessarily have to but
it definitely helps) you are bombarded every

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:33.050
second with reminders whether it’s billboards
or someone going by you, somebody else that’s

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:37.940
is higher up on the proverbial food chain
and it’s thrown in our face.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:45.620
It’s really wonderful to think of the unplugging
from that because this is almost the anti-culture

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.430
here.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:52.130
Was this something that was almost part of
your make-up or you had to learn this?

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:53.390
Erik: Oh no, I had to learn.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.841
Again it’s like an everyday practice (that
thing again), an everyday practice of I’m

00:03:57.841 --> 00:04:05.110
being about the purity of expression of growth
and learning and improving as opposed to as

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.570
opposed to how do I get the results that I
want from others.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.630
Because we don’t naturally do it that way,
we have to sort of like decide that’s how

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.280
I’m going to approach this instead.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.530
Film Courage: And also to in the last few
years (and not to get too morbid) but we’ve

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:26.180
seen people who have achieved tremendous success
obviously not be happy and then you have to

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.680
ask yourself well why am I doing this?

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.262
And it has to be (and as follow your bliss
bumper sticker on the back as it sounds) it

00:04:36.262 --> 00:04:41.320
has to be about that because we do see these
people who have achieved these amazing results

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.530
and the end result for them showed that it
wasn’t enough.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:51.590
Erik: I do think it really is about making
the daily practice/journey/writing whatever

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.290
enjoyable.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:58.820
Being at peace and enjoying what you’re
doing each day, finding a way to make it that

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:06.220
way so the journey is a positive journey and
not be so caught up in the destination because

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.970
if you enjoy what you’re doing you’re
going to do it more, you are going to do it

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.320
better, you are going to want to learn more.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:19.510
But it comes from a certain level of confidence
and trust that what you are doing is worthwhile

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:26.210
and if you measure that in only money or career
statistics you may never get there and you

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.530
may always be insecure about that.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:33.060
The people who want to write, there is usually
more of a compassion, more of a purity of

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.900
I just want to do this for some reason.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:43.860
So if you can really hold to that as opposed
to what the results are is what matters so

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.611
much you would probably have a better time
doing it, you will be happier, you’ll be

00:05:47.611 --> 00:05:53.620
more productive and prolific and the results
usually actually follow from that.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:06:00.060
Film Courage: Before you chose your major
for film and television what was your goal

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.139
or major before this?

00:06:02.139 --> 00:06:08.010
Erik: What I first started college, I experimented
with being an English major and thinking about

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.680
writing fiction, poetry.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.500
I experimented with being a music major briefly
but I pretty quickly took some introductory

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.370
film classes and I realized that this was
the medium that I wanted to try to write for.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.840
Film Courage: And you’ve said that [film]
festivals really aren’t about the laurels

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.730
(I heard you say this in another interview).

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:31.660
I think that’s actually beautifully put.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.590
Erik: I don’t think I said it but that’s
probably true.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:40.280
Film Courage: Okay, let’s say you didn’t
say it but this goes back to what we’re

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:44.690
talking about, the end result is not what
it should be about.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:49.590
And I know it’s easy to theorize that and
say these things and then you have to live

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:54.190
in a reality that is life and you’re competing
with people all the time and that’s something

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:59.030
that happens in pre-school and it happens
in adult hood but to really have something

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:03.790
that we get from it that is more than an accolade.

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:08.750
Do you think that is what keeps you going
in terms of why you do it?

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:11.780
Erik: I’m not sure what keeps me going to
be honest.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:15.710
I think like I keep going like most writers
because that’s just what I do.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.000
It’s what I’ve decided I do.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:23.590
It’s what I want to do and I’m engaged
with the challenge of getting better and writing

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.710
things that really fulfill why I wanted to
be a writer.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:31.270
They are the kind of things that I love that
I wanted to create more of in the world.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:35.669
And I guess part of that is to get it out
in the world I’m going to need other people

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:38.950
to help me do that and finance it or whatever.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:43.810
But it’s kind of like I haven’t achieved
yet what I wanted to achieve even though I’ve

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:48.680
worked on some big things and won awards and
made a living as a writer and stuff, there’s

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:56.140
a lot of kind of projects that I always got
into this because I wanted to do, that I still

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.240
want to do those.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.541
So I think there is just a desire to create
and express that never seems to really go

00:08:03.541 --> 00:08:08.780
away and so it’s just a chosen field.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.752
It keeps me going because it’s just the
path that I’m on and have chosen and have

00:08:12.752 --> 00:08:14.210
no reason to un-choose .

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:17.340
Film Courage: I can say the same for Film
Courage.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.190
It would be great to have videos get this
[certain] amount of views but at the end of

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:26.440
the day it has to be about conversations (like
today’s conversation) and that’s worth

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:27.440
it for us.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.660
That’s why we do this for.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:36.370
So even though I’m not a screenwriter, there
has to be something else besides hoping Erik’s

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:38.780
video gets millions of views.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.370
It has to be that the process was enjoyable
which it has been.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:43.370
Erik: Thank you.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:44.650
For me as well.

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.240
Film Courage: This is a great way to end.

